#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153050} :activity/id #uuid "888af819-3f31-5b7b-941a-2465f974729a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049133 :topic/id #uuid "dd88b5aa-842c-5755-bc2d-c71985927a3d" :topic/math-academy-id 2613} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152148 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151936 :question/id #uuid "260712d9-b48d-552e-a31a-0c2e74cafeb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6241"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30328c9f-afbd-5fd7-b32a-90717a813001" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "cf9d8974-1db3-5ee4-bd5f-2d355c9f3186" :step/math-academy-id 23446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152149 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151937 :question/id #uuid "07269d0a-faf3-5bf9-b397-8fc1574313a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6242"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bb0eba0-05b2-5908-9abc-74cddff4d1e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565423}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"} :step/id #uuid "bd7ec3fb-24ee-52f0-a2ed-3ccf5bf49da7" :step/math-academy-id 23447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153043}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153349 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will describe a process for calculating a **lower bound** for the volume enclosed between a surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane. \n\nAs an example, consider the rectangle $R$ in the $xy$-plane, defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sketch of the region $R$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bf7ef4752b0910b5544707eef97c854d8eeeedbc221a81c5fa631cf38b8b8d9a.png)\n\nNow consider the function $f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2,$ defined over $R,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/34/34f7936b29e6a177ae0c831e36654dc5059514580a1947324a40d52320b83e5a.png)\n\n \n\nThe space underneath the surface and above the region $R$ defines a fixed volume $V.$ Our goal is to find a lower bound for $V.$ We do this by approximating this volume using rectangular solids. \n\nWe start by partitioning the $x$- and $y$-intervals of $R.$ Let's pick two subintervals of equal length for both $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/c3/c375b605ca6b7d7f2935218976f5e09db5ed2cfb64d811b1fe0a854d02646a36.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is now partitioned into $4$ rectangles:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nWe seek a *lower* bound for the volume $V.$ So, we let $m_{ij}$ be the *smallest* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}.$ We then define a rectangular solid in each subregion whose base is $R_{ij}$ and whose height is $m_{ij}.$ \n\nFor example, the rectangular solid corresponding to $R_{11}$ is given below. \n\n![](images/43/4324b984fb1056750f9bfceda58d57b2e0dbf1875ff21479d5ef93f088bfc50b.png)\n\n \n\nThe volume of this rectangular solid is\n\n$$\nm_{11}\\cdot \\textrm{Area}(R_{11})  = \\dfrac14m_{11}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the volumes of the rectangular solids formed by the partitions $R_{21}, R_{12},$ and $R_{22}$ respectively are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac14m_{21}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{12}, \\qquad \\dfrac14m_{22}.\n$$\n\nDrawing all four rectangular solids, we get a volume that looks as follows:\n\n![](images/89/89d3706148d3f52096f94287c9cf4694b281bab18a8803bafe1b67d81e9b87fc.png)\n\n \n\nThe lower bound $L$ for our volume is the sum of the volumes of our four rectangular solids. This gives\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}).\n$$\n\nThe number $L$ is called the **lower Riemann sum with respect to the given partition**.\n\n**Note:** We don't yet know the values of $m_{ij},$ the height of each solid. We'll learn to figure these out a little later in the lesson. For now, we will just focus on writing $L$ as an expression of $m_{ij}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eda4b6f6-7ce7-5845-a288-86745288d9b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3953 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1f6e14ef-cfe2-5f79-a589-ff562b89f766" :step/math-academy-id 23456 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153416 :tutorial/content "Recall the region $R$ that we encountered earlier:\n\n![](images/20/2026210e2cd58b2f2f7290a2d4f4186875903d146d02597787393727afd7f6a6.png)\n\nEarlier, we saw that a lower bound for the volume $V$ of \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\ndefined over the region $R$ with respect to the partition $P$ (shown above) is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(m_{11} + m_{21}+ m_{12}+m_{22}),\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ over the subregion $R_{ij}.$ Our goal now is to calculate the values of $m_{ij}.$ \n\nWe start by considering $m_{11},$ so let's zoom in on $R_{11}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/04/042a236318a636de2fbfd9522dd221dbfd47906e63bc2290ca554bbcfccec958.png)\n\nNow, let's take a closer look at the function $f(x,y)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 3-x^2-y^2\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that\n\n- as we *increase* $x,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $x^2$), and\n\n- as we *increase* $y,$ the value of $f$ *decreases* (because we're *subtracting* $y^2$).\n\n![](images/4e/4e29408793329549172cdcc7113ec1d356d5b78e07b59b71e0d5284149375442.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in this subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of the subregion.\n\n![](images/13/13fb850ca6e17c9c68b29edd5b8f0a256a8099c9c1bf334270c5c90b4af98cc7.png)\n\nSince the function $f(x,y)$ decreases as $x$ and $y$ increase everywhere on $R,$ the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$ *always* occurs at the top right corner.\n\n![](images/92/9271ba6c0cb7d36b1480da239efb4896a715c3b0df244ebba7545486fe47dafd.png)\n\nCalculating the value of $f$ at each top right corner, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=2.5 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(1,\\frac{1}{2})=1.75 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(\\frac{1}{2},1)=1.75 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(1,1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above values in our expression for $L,$ we get\n\n$$\nL = \\dfrac14(2.5+1.75+1.75+1) = 1.75.\n$$\n\nRemember that $L$ is the *lower* Riemann sum, which is a *lower* bound for the volume between $z = f(x,y)$ and the region $R.$ Therefore, we conclude that the volume between $z = f(x,y)$ and the region $R$ must be greater than (or equal to) $1.75.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe50d6fe-4440-5c53-9c52-413256b1f114" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5775 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Minimum Value of the Function in Each Partition"} :step/id #uuid "7f3422a5-af38-55ce-9753-7b1b845c12a9" :step/math-academy-id 35472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153184}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152231 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152041 :question/id #uuid "60ec2df0-87b9-544f-b286-2ed5e17d92fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-9476"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6123a27-2dc8-5076-8c77-1bf056f46566" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049878}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565462}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Expression for the Lower Bounds"} :step/id #uuid "1dfceb2d-adf2-5017-aaab-f12ee95af9ab" :step/math-academy-id 35473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153181}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153184 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153418 :tutorial/content "We need to introduce just a bit more notation. To illustrate, let's consider the region $R$ and the partition $P$ shown below.\n\n![](images/5d/5d5af7cb2f2b12a52bb624d12938aafd2d34b079ad5d28dc44133292b98956ec.png)\n\nIt's convenient to express our region $R$ and partition $P$ in a way that does not involve a diagram. We can do this using the Cartesian product.\n\nUsing the Cartesian product, we have $R = [1,4]\\times [1,3].$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- The partition of the $x$-interval $[1,4]$ can be expressed as $P_1 = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.$\n\n- The partition of the $y$-interval $[1,3]$ can be expressed as $P_2 = \\{1,3\\}.$\n\n- Therefore, we can write the partition as $P = P_1\\times P_2.$\n\nFinally, we write $L(f,P)$ for the lower Riemann sum of the function $f$ with respect to $P.$\n\n**Note:** We write $L(f,P)$ because the answer we get for the lower bound $L$ depends on the function $f$ and the partition $P$ that we choose. If we choose a different function or partition, then we'll get a different lower bound." :tutorial/id #uuid "445f66a1-3273-53e7-aba4-f3b3c5016df5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5781 :tutorial/title "Updating Our Notation"} :step/id #uuid "3fde6076-3c6b-56e6-8d2d-2d6c3868eb85" :step/math-academy-id 35506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153044}}] :activity/title "Approximating Volumes Using Lower Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152325}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552529} :activity/id #uuid "ae59c15e-8f64-5d6d-946d-9c2353f25611" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049133 :topic/id #uuid "dd88b5aa-842c-5755-bc2d-c71985927a3d" :topic/math-academy-id 2613} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552529 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51e27ca0-b1f0-501d-8d8a-15d892923c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342916} {:answer/id #uuid "69fa31fd-299d-521f-935b-b7bc44d987ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343143} {:answer/id #uuid "9011bf66-d25a-5f40-81fc-8d7cc4f3a6e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343495} {:answer/id #uuid "e1dece52-db3a-5167-887d-cab95aa2752c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344226} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7ce6f3-9dae-58d1-a27e-ed19aa7aaaab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344466}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342916} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7e2232-ee64-5a5c-ba90-c8733c5e655d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "591dbb3d-401d-5ba1-98ad-e1bc0718cda4" :question/math-academy-id "q-75248" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=x+y-1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/3b/3be0ba3fa44ba61e0b55500e588387cd087b8d2f2687c5636c9b053f1f8dc5d6.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=x+y-1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/b3/b3c2a3891860a8ad88d2f111a3e279a65398a37020ab2f0843f8475dfd38df15.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/76/76cbbcf7ddff625cf035d6f7e37acbc6d278734cc87648ef6a88f8ace61f731e.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,0)=0 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,1)=1 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =0+1+1+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02ba616f-857b-5b1b-a652-302fd8658f7d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552572}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e05b1e5-a40e-54fd-9b04-d5dca64f71b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254083} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfd14db-f28a-5907-bae0-c07e9ca18570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254341} {:answer/id #uuid "5616af10-5723-542b-9fd4-3b9b76730524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254427} {:answer/id #uuid "922d36ec-1c65-5bf2-9934-84d3bfa56c5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254951} {:answer/id #uuid "f392c96c-38da-5421-a0e9-e7d801f6a160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254083} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbc83de8-3399-50c2-a721-1d19605f5c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acabb8ff-4a50-5eab-8825-59e98be4e24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-122963" :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=10-2x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{1,3\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/40/40eb21cbafdbd721ac8f1710d52918d5e463fa3ffc6eb06aeba8539eb53c7db8.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=10-2x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/c1/c17b413125919726cd1aba9efdc4dcb5949fee52947610abcbb952f1b7917281.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/5d/5dbf341642f5e15942323ec3d5674fc361f0b48d9cabd5f54c7fedb95a3f6fb6.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(3,1)=3 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(3,2)=2 \\\\ {m}_{31} & =f(3,3)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1=12.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "14825d79-a165-5a8f-bd7f-a28da631103e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7999b8-b233-5c53-8825-7de78eea7f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253808} {:answer/id #uuid "260fa322-ead1-5a86-9218-41aaf1516f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d99e890-0a44-5df1-af7d-00eeaad8fc09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255055} {:answer/id #uuid "a13c9ea8-e7c6-5b02-81b7-87b7c9a73a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255088} {:answer/id #uuid "cc63917c-9227-514c-861c-c65220955f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255487} :answer-field/id #uuid "17b5d493-1477-5182-82d2-93e08771b9bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5b65c6c-6196-523c-833d-8bae70c92a8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-122983" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [0,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{0,1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/73/7310b1302c35084aaef5a8b2316f59c0fbd806bc9314a51fe77167988d01f343.png)\n\nThe lower sum $L(f,P)$ is given by \n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{13}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{13})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=2$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=2{m}_{11}+2{m}_{12}+2{m}_{13}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y+1$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/ce/ce58eb1c2ee59cf97aa77fb9e840578e7a99f094b11b2e282317aad69ae31b7a.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/ce/cec70986dea862392342377d83adbfa534ce0f3aa87cf2161c2f88eb83fbed58.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(0,0)=1 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(0,1)=2 \\\\ {m}_{13} & =f(0,2)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & =2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6946aa2b-6720-5a06-abdf-e444f41a9ede"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "258f88c7-c0a2-56e8-9230-059a60c0a8e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551751} {:answer/id #uuid "858f5818-4647-5518-b75d-f2e9dd90e7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551985} {:answer/id #uuid "98578764-7601-57dc-9d67-e947a3ce724a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552034} {:answer/id #uuid "b17bf7f2-0d3c-57ff-a579-7f0faaabe3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552094} {:answer/id #uuid "ec56fda9-5a7a-576c-a3c2-f64506f5aa01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551751} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7df747d-5ae6-580b-89b5-66c235ad174c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a55a479e-4a45-5971-8018-f156cd1ee741" :question/math-academy-id "q-75252" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,4]\\times [1,5]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=9-x-y.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,2,4\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,3,5\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/97/97220aa0d23d01e807608e6194a37661ba77cc391d0adf537908c1f13b1b6de8.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=4$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4{m}_{11}+4{m}_{21}+4{m}_{12}+4{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=9-x-y$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/30/306666a0e69fca9dc47c2295aa616a944482f00b28fdd96d9d96af816b058447.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/15/15512c5f5063309474534a54b323d87c28cf9b776c10789a8f70e12cdb898aeb.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(2,3)=4 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(4,3)=2 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(2,5)=2 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(4,5)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=4\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0=32.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "80f7dff7-91ed-5deb-8aec-a5448ca37634" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552617}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57a03b7d-8141-5d28-a86e-2499e1398aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342970} {:answer/id #uuid "6e80cc3e-2714-55da-bfca-0054503bf050" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343188} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba6b947-6e76-544f-ad92-7e25df4c2244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343300} {:answer/id #uuid "f3915db4-6e1c-5806-94b9-0e54df842dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344380} {:answer/id #uuid "ff44fa67-8e68-5cb5-bb53-95c06c461de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344380} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2394566-b7eb-566f-8fd0-125f8b6baf2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a7c41781-bde2-5bb7-ab42-7938c0b39776" :question/math-academy-id "q-75255" :question/problem "Let $R=[0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{1}=\\{0,1,2\\},\\,{P}_{2}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,P={P}_{1}\\times {P}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the lower Riemann sum $L(f,P).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](images/4f/4faf932e63a57d2fe53fb3942cfe21c65d0d2a88692c665b2706bd68164855c9.png)\n\nThe lower Riemann sum $L(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{m}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{m}_{12}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{12})+{m}_{22}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{22})\n$$\n\nwhere ${m}_{ij}$ is the minimum value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *minimum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *lower* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=1$ for all $4$ subregions, the formula for $L(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\nL(f,P)={m}_{11}+{m}_{21}+{m}_{12}+{m}_{22}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=13-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](images/e7/e70e2cf07b27a91c34e59435229302f3b2127b15a2f39049c0a8389cd6f59863.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](images/aa/aa3858dba149ebf5650ed06f75444244494fe0a961f897100914d54f6d7c0b49.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{11} & =f(1,2)=8 \\\\ {m}_{21} & =f(2,2)=5 \\\\ {m}_{12} & =f(1,3)=3 \\\\ {m}_{22} & =f(2,3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nL(f,P)=8+5+3+0=16.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb90fa3f-df3e-542a-90df-f14f7ae73efa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552538}}] :activity/title "Approximating Volumes Using Lower Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551658}]}
